<Base title="Disclaimer" description="Disclaimer of Talha M Blog: our content is for information only — how we handle accuracy, allegations, sources, reader comments and corrections.">
  <section class="article-head"><h1>Disclaimer</h1></section>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><em>Last updated: October 15, 2026</em></p>

    <p>This Disclaimer explains the limits of the information published on Talha M Blog (talhablog.com). Please read it together with our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a> and <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>. By using this website, you acknowledge and accept this disclaimer.</p>

    <h2>General information only</h2>
    <p>Everything published on Talha M Blog — news explanations, background articles, lists, guides and summaries — is provided for general information and educational purposes only. We write to help ordinary readers understand events and topics. Nothing on this website is published as professional advice, an official statement, or a substitute for the original documents and authorities involved in a story.</p>

    <h2>Not professional advice</h2>
    <p>Talha M Blog does not provide legal, medical, financial, investment or any other professional advice. Some of our stories mention health, medicines, money, business, law or court cases because those subjects appear in the news. Reporting on such topics is not a recommendation. If you face a real decision about your health, your money, a legal matter or your safety, please consult a qualified and licensed professional or an official authority in your own country, who can consider your personal situation. Acting only on a simplified news explanation would be unwise, and we say so openly.</p>

    <h2>Accuracy, completeness and developing stories</h2>
    <p>We check facts against reliable sources before publishing, and for important stories we compare reports from at least three independent sources. However, news is a moving target: eyewitness accounts differ, official figures are revised, charges are amended, and stories that look settled in the morning can change by the evening. We therefore cannot guarantee that every article is perfectly accurate, complete or current at the moment you read it. Where a detail is reported but not yet confirmed, we try to mark it clearly inside the story instead of presenting it as settled fact.</p>
    <p>When we discover an error, or a reader reports one and it checks out, we correct the article and show an "Updated" date on it. If you believe anything on this website is wrong, please tell us through our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a> — corrections genuinely help every future reader.</p>

    <h2>Allegations, court cases and right of reply</h2>
    <p>Some of our stories report on crimes, arrests, trials and disputes. In such stories, charges and claims are <b>allegations</b> unless and until a court decides otherwise, and every person is presumed innocent until proven guilty. When we write that a person was arrested, charged or accused, we are reporting what officials, courts or other sources have said — we are not declaring that the person did what they are accused of. Where reports conflict, or a claim rests on unnamed sources, we say so inside the story.</p>
    <p>If you, or an organisation you represent, are mentioned in a story and you believe something in it is wrong or unfair, you have a right of reply. Email us at <a href="mailto:contact@talhablog.com">contact@talhablog.com</a> with the link of the story and your response. We will check it promptly and, where it is fair, correct the story or add your response to it.</p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>Our stories are based on reports from news agencies, newspapers, official statements and other sources, which we name at the end of each story. Our newer stories also link to the original reports, so readers can check them directly, and we are adding links to older stories gradually. Naming or linking a source does not mean we agree with everything that source publishes, and a source report can itself later be corrected by its publisher.</p>

    <h2>Views and neutrality</h2>
    <p>Reporting about a person, company, party, government or event does not mean that Talha M Blog supports or opposes them. Quotations and claims that appear inside our stories belong to the people and organisations that made them; we report them so readers can understand a story, not because we personally endorse every claim. Our aim is explanation, not campaigning.</p>

    <h2>Reader comments</h2>
    <p>The comments under our stories are written by readers, and they express the views of the person who posted them — not the views of Talha M Blog. We moderate comments and may hide or remove comments that break the comment rules in our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>, but we cannot check every comment before it appears, and we are not responsible for what readers write. If you see a comment that is abusive, false or unlawful, please email us with the link of the story and we will review it.</p>

    <h2>How our stories are prepared</h2>
    <p>We research every story from published sources and write it in our own words. Like many modern publishers, we sometimes use AI writing tools to help prepare drafts. Every story is checked against reliable sources and edited before publishing, and the editor of this website takes responsibility for what is published. We never knowingly publish invented quotations, invented statistics or invented events.</p>

    <h2>Images are illustrations</h2>
    <p>The pictures published with our stories are illustrations created for Talha M Blog to represent the subject of a story. They are not real press photographs, and they should not be treated as visual proof of a person, place or event. In the rare case that a real photograph from another source is ever used, it will be credited on the page.</p>

    <h2>External links</h2>
    <p>Our articles link to outside websites so that readers can see original reports, official statements and further reading. We do not control those websites, they can change or disappear at any time, and their content is the responsibility of their owners. A link is provided for information; it is not a guarantee that everything on the linked site is accurate, safe or suitable for you.</p>

    <h2>Products, prices and tools</h2>
    <p>Stories sometimes mention products, phones, apps, prices or services because they are part of the news. Prices and offers change quickly and differ from country to country and shop to shop. A mention is never a promise about a price or quality, and it is not personalised advice to buy or avoid anything. Always check the current details with the seller or provider before making a decision.</p>

    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>This website displays advertising served by Google AdSense. Advertisements are selected automatically by Google and may be based on your browsing, as explained in our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>. The appearance of an advertisement on Talha M Blog does not mean that we have tested, verified or endorsed the advertised product, service or company. Any purchase or agreement you make with an advertiser is entirely between you and them.</p>

    <h2>Use at your own judgement</h2>
    <p>To the extent allowed by law, Talha M Blog accepts no responsibility for decisions taken or losses suffered based on information read on this website. We work hard to be accurate and fair, and we correct our mistakes openly — but readers should always weigh important information against official and primary sources before acting on it.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about this Disclaimer, or reports of mistakes, are welcome through our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a> or by email at <a href="mailto:contact@talhablog.com">contact@talhablog.com</a>.</p>
